  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "We have taken a great initiative to have a women only quota for admissions. The admission criteria is to solve one graph problem. Alice, being a woman, seeks admission under this quota and wants your help to solve the following problem.\n\nYou are given an integer NNN. Construct a good graph with NNN vertices.\n\nA good graph is one that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n\nThe graph is simple (i.e, doesn't contain self-loops or multiedges), undirected, and **connected**.\nIf ∣i−j∣|i-j|∣i−j∣ is a prime number, then degi≠degjdeg_i \\neq deg_jdegi​=degj​, where degideg_idegi​ is the degree of vertex iii.\nAmong all graphs on NNN vertices that satisfy the above two conditions, a good graph is one that then **minimizes the maximum degree**, i.e, max⁡i=1Ndegi\\max_{i=1}^N deg_imaxi=1N​degi​ is minimized.\nIf such a graph exists, find any one of them. If no such graph exists, print −1-1−1.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nThe first and only line of each test case contains a single integer NNN — the number of nodes in the graph.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output the answer as follows:\n\n\n\nIf no good graph on NNN vertices exists, print −1-1−1 on a new line.\nOtherwise, first print a single integer XXX: the maximum degree of a good graph on NNN vertices.\nOn the next line, print a single integer MMM: the number of edges in the graph you construct.\nThen, print MMM lines. The iii-th of these lines should contain two space-separated integers uiu_iui​ and viv_ivi​, denoting an edge between uiu_iui​ and viv_ivi​.\n\nThe graph you print should satisfy all the conditions laid out in the statement.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\leq T \\leq 1001≤T≤100",
      "2≤N≤10002 \\leq N \\leq 10002≤N≤1000",
      "The sum of NNN over all test cases won't exceed 500050005000."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2\n3\n4",
        "output": "1\n1\n1 2\n2\n2\n1 3\n2 3\n2\n3\n1 2\n2 3\n1 4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: N=2N = 2N=2, and we have deg1=deg2=1deg_1 = deg_2 = 1deg1​=deg2​=1 which is the best we can do.\nTest case 222: N=3N = 3N=3, and we have deg1=deg2=1deg_1 = deg_2 = 1deg1​=deg2​=1 and deg3=2deg_3 = 2deg3​=2. ∣1−3∣=2|1-3| = 2∣1−3∣=2 is prime, and 111 and 333 have different degrees as required.\nTest case 333: N=4N = 4N=4, and we have deg1=deg2=2deg_1 = deg_2 = 2deg1​=deg2​=2, deg3=deg4=1deg_3 = deg_4 = 1deg3​=deg4​=1. It's easy to verify that any pair with a difference of 222 or 333 have different degrees."
      }
